[Chapter "Key ideas"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bqr1k1/bpp2pp1/p1np1n1p/4p3/4P3/1BPP1N1P/PP3PP1/R1BQRNK1 b - - 3 11"]

{[%cal Gd3d4,Gf1g3,Bg3f5] [%csl Yf5] Build slowly, then open the center with d4 once your pieces are ready. Pawns on c3 and d3 support e4 and prepare d4. The bishop stays on the a2-g8 diagonal, dropping back to b3 or c2 when attacked. Castle, then Re1 guards e4. The queen's knight travels b1-d2-f1-g3, where it eyes f5 and h5. h3 stops ...Bg4 and ...Ng4. If Black breaks early with ...d5, hit e5 and keep developing.} *

[Chapter "Main line: 3...Bc5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{Black develops the bishop to its most active square, aiming at f2. We answer with the slow, flexible Giuoco Pianissimo.}
1. e4 {Takes the center and opens lines for the bishop and queen.} e5
2. Nf3 {Develops with an attack on e5.} Nc6
3. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] The Italian bishop targets f7, the weakest point near Black's king.} Bc5
4. c3 {[%cal Gd2d4] Prepares d4 and clears c2 as a retreat square for the bishop.} Nf6
5. d3 {Guards e4 calmly. We keep d4 in reserve for the right moment.} d6
(5... O-O 6. O-O d5 {Black strikes in the center at once.} 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5,Gf3e5] Now e5 is attacked twice, so Black must spend a move defending it while we develop.})
6. O-O O-O
(6... a6 7. a4 {Gains queenside space and stops ...b5 from chasing our bishop.} Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 {Same setup as the main line; the knight heads for g3.})
7. Re1 {Protects e4 and supports a later d4.} a6 {Gives the bishop a safe retreat on a7 when we play d4 or b4.}
(7... Ng4 {Eyes f2, but a lone knight can't do much.} 8. Re2 {Covers f2 calmly.} Kh8 9. h3 Nf6 10. Nbd2 {The knight has gone back, and h3 is a useful move for us anyway.})
8. Bb3 {Steps back before ...b5 or ...Na5 can hit the bishop, keeping it on the same diagonal.} Ba7
9. h3 {[%csl Yg4] Takes g4 away from Black's bishop and knight.} h6
10. Nbd2 Re8
11. Nf1 {[%cal Gf1g3] The knight heads for g3, where it eyes f5 and h5.} Be6 {Offers to trade off our best bishop.}
12. Bc2 {Keeps the bishop. On c2 it still aims at the kingside and supports e4.} d5
13. exd5 Bxd5
14. Ng3 {Development is complete. Next comes Nh4-f5 or d4, depending on Black's setup.} *

[Chapter "Two Knights: 3...Nf6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Two Knights Defense, Modern Bishop's Opening"]
[ECO "C55"]

{3...Nf6 attacks e4. We defend calmly with d3 and keep the same setup.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6
4. d3 {Protects e4. The sharp 4.Ng5 leads to heavy theory; 4.d3 keeps us on familiar ground.} Be7
(4... Bc5 5. c3 {Transposes to the main line.})
(4... h6 {Rules out Ng5 and Bg5 ideas.} 5. O-O d6 6. c3 g6 {Black fianchettoes the bishop.} 7. Re1 Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Nf1 {Same plan: Ng3, h3, then d4.})
5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 Na5 {Black hunts our bishop.}
8. Bb5 {Avoids the trade. The bishop will drop back to a4 and then c2.} a6
9. Ba4 b5 10. Bc2 {Safe on c2 and still aiming at the kingside.} c5 {Black gains queenside space, Ruy Lopez style.}
11. Nbd2 Nc6 12. Nf1 {The knight goes to g3 next, and d4 comes when we are ready.} *

[Chapter "Other third moves"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Hungarian Defense"]
[ECO "C50"]

{Three quieter third moves. Against each, the same setup works.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Be7 {The Hungarian Defense: solid but passive.}
(3... h6 {Prevents Ng5 but costs a move.} 4. d3 Nf6 5. c3 d6 6. O-O g6 7. Re1 Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Nf1 {Our usual plan: Ng3, h3, then d4.})
(3... d6 {Blocks Black's own bishop on f8.} 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 {[%engine ok] Consistent with our setup. The sharper 5.Ng5 hits f7 and is objectively stronger.} Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1)
4. d3 Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {The same structure as the Two Knights chapter.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Blackburne Shilling Gambit"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Blackburne-Kostić Gambit"]
[ECO "C50"]

{3...Nd4 sets a famous trap. Take the knight, not the pawn, and watch for ...Bc5.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nd4 {Leaves e5 hanging on purpose.}
4. Nxd4 {Trades off the strong knight. White is already better.}
(4. Nxe5? {Greedy.} Qg5! {Hits the knight and g2 at once.} 5. Nxf7? Qxg2 6. Rf1 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 Nf3# {Mate by knight. This is the trap Black was hoping for.})
4... exd4 5. O-O {Castle first, then go after the pawn on d4.} Bc5? {Natural, but it walks into a fork.}
(5... Nf6 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {Undermines d4.} Be7 8. cxd4 {Wins the pawn Black left on d4.} c6 9. d5 cxd5 10. exd5 O-O 11. Nc3 {A clean extra pawn and easy development.})
6. Bxf7+! {[%cal Gd1h5] Pulls the king onto the same diagonal as our queen.} Kxf7
7. Qh5+ {[%cal Gh5c5] Check, and the queen also attacks the bishop on c5.} g6
(7... Kf8 8. Qxc5+ d6 9. Qxd4 {Two pawns up, and Black can no longer castle.})
8. Qxc5 d5 9. Qxd4 {Two pawns up with the safer king.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the early ...Ng4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{Black's knight jumps to g4 to attack f2. Don't defend passively: strike in the center.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 Ng4?! {[%cal Rg4f2,Rc5f2] Attacks f2 with knight and bishop.}
6. d4! {[%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5] Hits the bishop and the e5 pawn while Black's knight is far from the center.}
(6. O-O {Also good.} Nxf2? 7. Rxf2 Bxf2+ 8. Kxf2 {Two minor pieces for a rook and pawn: a good trade for White in the middlegame.})
6... exd4
(6... Bd6 {Keeps e5, but the bishop now blocks Black's d-pawn.} 7. h3 Nf6 {The knight went to g4 and back, and the bishop blocks the d-pawn. Black has simply lost time.})
7. cxd4 Bb4+ 8. Nc3 O-O 9. O-O {Our pawns rule the center, and the knight on g4 has no good squares.} *
